course: euphemisms
lesson: 12
title: Soft words for hard things: pregnancy and birth and relations and family
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Families use soft words for things people feel shy or sad about: a hurried wedding, where babies come from, a couple who split up.
explain: Old-fashioned soft words hide gossip politely («a seven-month baby»), funny ones put off awkward questions («found under a cabbage leaf»).
explain: Words about couples are often vague on purpose: «partner» or «friend» can mean a boyfriend or girlfriend, and «it didn't work out» means a relationship ended.
explain: Vivid example: a film star announces a «conscious uncoupling», which sounds like a science experiment but simply means a divorce.
fact: "Shotgun wedding" means a wedding hurried because a baby is on the way (jokey).
fact: "Seven-month baby" was an old gossip phrase for a baby that came less than nine months after the wedding.
fact: "Found under a cabbage leaf" is a jokey story told to small children about where babies come from (UK).
fact: "Her time came" means she went into labour and the baby began to be born (old-fashioned).
fact: "A little stranger" means a baby that is expected (old-fashioned, UK).
fact: "Partner" means a boyfriend, girlfriend or someone you live with without marrying.
fact: "Friend" can politely mean a boyfriend or girlfriend.
fact: "Companion" means a long-term partner who is not a husband or wife (old-fashioned).
fact: "A couple" means two people in a romantic relationship.
fact: "Seeing someone" means dating someone.
fact: "An item" means two people are in a relationship.
fact: "Going steady" means dating one person seriously (old-fashioned, US).
fact: "Between relationships" means single.
fact: "Between marriages" means divorced and not married again (jokey).
fact: "Separated" means a married couple living apart, before or instead of a divorce.
fact: "Living apart" means married but no longer living together.
fact: "Conscious uncoupling" means a divorce or break-up (jokey, from a famous celebrity).
fact: "Parted ways" means broke up.
fact: "It didn't work out" means the marriage or relationship ended.
fact: "Broken home" is an old-fashioned phrase for a family where the parents divorced.
exercise: What is a "shotgun wedding"? | A wedding hurried because a baby is coming | A wedding with fireworks and a loud band at night | A wedding held at a shooting club | A very loud wedding party
exercise: An old aunt sniffs that the baby was "a seven-month baby." What is she hinting? | The baby came too soon after the wedding | The baby was born very small and weak in winter | The baby was seven months old | The baby could walk at seven months
exercise: Gran tells a toddler he was "found under a cabbage leaf." What is she doing? | Avoiding explaining where babies come from | Telling him he was adopted from another family | Telling him about her vegetable garden | Telling him he was lost outside
exercise: In an old book, "her time came" in the night. What happened? | She went into labour | She died | Her clock stopped | She woke up early
exercise: An old letter speaks of "a little stranger" expected in June. What is expected? | A baby | A foreign visitor | A new servant | A lost puppy
exercise: Miss Lee says, "My partner is picking me up." Who is the partner? | The person she is in a relationship with | Her business colleague from the same office | Her tennis teammate | Her taxi driver
exercise: Uncle Ray brings "a friend" to every family dinner and holds her hand. What is she? | His girlfriend | His neighbour | His workmate | His cousin
exercise: An old obituary (a newspaper notice about someone who has died) says he left behind "his companion of thirty years." Who is that? | His long-term partner he never married | His old dog | His best schoolfriend from his village days | His nurse
exercise: "Are Tom and Ella a couple?" What is being asked? | Whether they are in a romantic relationship | Whether they are twins | Whether they always sit together at the same desk | Whether there are two of them
exercise: "My sister is seeing someone." What does that mean? | She is dating someone | She has good eyesight | She visits a doctor | She saw a stranger
exercise: "Are those two an item now?" What is being asked? | Whether they are in a relationship | Whether they are for sale in a shop window | Whether they are on a list | Whether they are in the same team
exercise: Grandpa says he and Grandma were "going steady" at sixteen. What does he mean? | They were dating each other seriously | They walked carefully | They drove slowly | They worked side by side in the same village shop
exercise: A singer says she is "between relationships." What is she? | Single | Engaged | Married | Divorced twice
exercise: Uncle Pete jokes that he is "between marriages." What is he really? | Divorced and not married again | Planning a big wedding for the summer | Living with two wives | A wedding photographer
exercise: "Mum and Dad are separated." What does that mean? | They are married but live apart | They are in different rooms of the house | They are on different teams | They were born apart
exercise: "My parents are living apart now." What has happened? | They no longer live together | They moved to a bigger house | They moved abroad together | They are on holiday in two places
exercise: A celebrity announces a "conscious uncoupling." What is it? | A divorce or break-up | A meditation class | A train being split | A new science show
exercise: "We parted ways last summer," says the singer about her husband. What happened? | They broke up | They walked different paths on a hike | They moved to different cities for work | They went on separate holidays
exercise: "We were married five years but it didn't work out." What happened? | The marriage ended | The wedding was cancelled | They lost their jobs | They failed an exam together
exercise: An old school report says a boy "comes from a broken home." What does it mean? | His parents are divorced | His house is damaged | His family is poor | His home has no roof
test: Gossip at the shop: "Shotgun wedding, that was. Baby came in the autumn." What is the gossip saying? | They married quickly because a baby was coming | Someone fired a gun into the air at the wedding | The wedding was in the autumn | The baby was at the wedding
test: Little Lily: "Mummy says I came from under a cabbage!" What is true? | Mummy used a funny story instead of explaining | Lily was really found in a garden | Lily was born in a vegetable patch | Lily is a gardener
test: Tabloid headline: "Pop star and actor ARE an item!" What does the headline claim? | The two are in a relationship | The two are selling something | The two are on a list | The two are in the same film
test: Text: "u still seeing that guy from football?" What is being asked? | Whether you are still dating him | Whether you can still see him on the pitch | Whether he still plays | Whether he needs glasses
test: Nan's diary, 1962: "Bill and I are going steady at last!" What happened? | Bill and Nan became a serious couple | Bill learned to drive carefully and slowly | They both got jobs | They stopped running
test: TV host: "So, are you single?" Singer: "Let's say I'm between relationships." What is the true answer? | Yes, she is single | No, she is married | She has two partners | She is engaged
test: Classmate: "My mum and dad are separated, but they're not divorced." What is true? | They are married but live apart | They live together happily | They are divorced already | They have never met
test: Press release: "After much thought, we have decided to consciously uncouple." What really happened? | The couple are splitting up | They joined a yoga class together | They had a baby | They got married
test: Grumpy uncle: "Him and his missus? Didn't work out, did it. She's gone." What happened? | The marriage ended | The car broke down | The house was sold | The holiday was cancelled
test: Old novel: "In the small hours, her time came, and the midwife was sent for." What was happening? | She was starting to give birth | She was dying | She was late for her work at the shop | She was waking up
test: Aunt in a letter: "Expecting a little stranger at Christmas!" What is she telling? | She is expecting a baby | A guest from abroad will visit | She bought a new doll | A lost dog will arrive
test: Teacher's old note: "Comes from a broken home, be gentle with him." What does the note mean? | His parents have divorced | His house was damaged | He breaks things at home | He is homeless
exam: Identify the meaning most accurately conveyed by the statement that two persons have "parted ways". | Their relationship has been terminated | They have travelled in opposing directions | They have dissolved a business partnership | They have concluded a walking excursion
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a "seven-month baby", as the expression was formerly employed. | A child born less than nine months after the parents' marriage | An infant born at seven months of gestation for medical reasons | A child of seven months of age | An infant capable of walking at seven months
exam: It is stated that a public figure is accompanied by "her companion of many years". Ascertain the nature of the relationship thereby indicated. | A long-standing partner to whom she is not married | A paid employee providing care | A domestic animal | A childhood acquaintance
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a couple described as "living apart"? | They are married but no longer reside together | They have relocated jointly to another region | They have never cohabited prior to marriage | They are temporarily travelling abroad
